          <p>
            Let's be honest about how most private tuition teachers track fees right now:
            a diary, a notebook, or a rough WhatsApp note to themselves.
            It works — until it doesn't.
          </p>

          <p>
            By the third week of the month, the picture gets messy.
            Rahul paid something last Tuesday — was it the full amount?
            Ananya's parents said they'd transfer it — did they?
            And the new student who joined on the 15th — what's their monthly fee even supposed to be?
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            "Sir next week pakka de denge" — and somehow, three weeks have passed.
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            The problem isn't that teachers are disorganized. The problem is that
            <strong>the tools don't match the job.</strong>
            A diary is fine for notes. It's not designed for keeping a proper
            student fee record format across 15 students and 12 months.
          </p>

          <p>
            That's exactly what a well-built <strong>tuition fee register format</strong> solves.
            And if you're not ready for a full digital system yet,
            a good Excel sheet is the right next step.
          </p>

